{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792927848,"nonce":"YVD6cbcyllWwu3fyA9xxaLPJD+c=","title":"zypy speed table: the count of kernels ahead of PyPy swings with the runner's CPU","message":"The Linux table's lead count went 12 -\u003e 16 -\u003e 14 -\u003e 10 of 22 over four runs whose zypy code did not change in release between the last three (39cdefea..5dbc8673 touched only a debug-only SSA check, the snapshot cache's rename, a conformance list and a test). Each run got a different CPU, recorded in the artifact's meta:\n\n| commit | run | CPU | ahead |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| a2f8b717 | 39940638103 | AMD EPYC 7763 | 12/22 |\n| 39cdefea | 40149017112 | Intel Xeon Platinum 8573C | 16/22 |\n| 5321f901 | 40173953647 | AMD EPYC 9V74 | 14/22 |\n| 5dbc8673 | 40184462883 | AMD EPYC 9V45 | 10/22 |\n\nOn the Xeon every time was 1.7 to 1.9x slower than on the 9V45, PyPy's more than zypy's on some kernels (ai: PyPy 31.5 vs 12.0 ms, zypy 30.6 vs 16.5), so kernels near parity flip: ai 0.97 -\u003e 1.37, crypto_pyaes 0.67 -\u003e 1.02, nbody 0.94 -\u003e 1.08, raytrace 0.97 -\u003e 1.52, richards 0.96 -\u003e 1.04, spectral-norm 0.96 -\u003e 1.80, sparsematmult 0.91 -\u003e 1.32; float went the other way, 1.07 -\u003e 0.51. macOS (one runner type) held at 14 to 15.\n\nThe table should name the CPU it was measured on and compare a run only with runs on the same CPU; a count across CPUs says nothing about a change. Interleaving the zypy and PyPy reps within a run would also narrow each ratio.","files":null}]}